  "query_preview": "..(Monday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 213 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThose who have been looking forward to pleasant autumn weather \nacross Eastern Kentucky are in luck, because the long term forecast \nperiod is just that. When the period opens Monday night, a drier \nand cooler airmass will continue to be advecting into the region \nvia northerly to northwesterly flow throughout the atmospheric \ncolumn. Shifting synoptics aloft will cause the mid/upper level \nflow to adopt a quasi- zonal orientation by midweek, and the \ncorresponding surface anticyclone is expected to propagate east \nthrough the Wabash and Ohio River Valleys in this same time frame.\nWhile mostly clear skies and the loss of the northerly wind \ncomponent favor a warming trend, neither of these shifts favor \nefficient moisture return. Rather, they favor a prolonged period \nof efficient diurnal insolation and mixing processes in Eastern \nKentucky. \n\nIn sensible weather terms, this means that widespread high \ntemperatures in the 70s on Monday and Tuesday will creep towards the \n80 degree mark as the week progresses. Southern and western locales \nwill be the first to return to highs in the 80s on Wednesday, with \nthese above-normal high temperatures creeping further east-northeast \non Thursday and again Friday. However, dewpoints will likely \nstruggle to rise much higher than the low to mid 50s. Efficient \ndiurnal mixing should yield dewpoint depressions on the magnitude of \n25-35 degrees, and it is plausible that those dewpoints could under-\nperform the blended NBM guidance used to populate the long term \nforecast grids. Nevertheless, with fair skies in the forecast grids, \nit should feel fantastic outside each afternoon. These are prime \nconditions for outdoor fall activities such as hiking and leaf-\nchasing, so get outside and enjoy it! \n\nThis week's synoptic weather pattern is also favorable for efficient \nradiational cooling at night. Expect ridge/valley temperature splits \nto emerge after sunset each night, with the shadier valleys dropping \noff the fastest. Some of the most sheltered/shaded valley locales \ncould experience lows in the upper 30s on Monday and Tuesday nights, \nbut most valley locations should experience MinTs in the 40s this \nweek. Fog remains favored over frost as a result, with the greatest \nfog coverage expected along the area's waterways. Therefore, aside \nfrom localized visibility reductions from the AM fog, the risk for \nhazardous weather in the long term forecast period is quite low.",
